You're diving into the world of tactics, techniques, and procedures (TTPs) used by adversaries. T1190, specifically, refers to the exploitation of public-facing applications to gain unauthorized access to a system or network.

In our case, let's take a look at the web server running on this system. We're currently running `nginx/1.24.0` on Ubuntu 22.04.3 LTS, with kernel 5.15.0-91. The web server is configured to listen on port 80 (HTTP) and 443 (HTTPS). You can verify this by checking the `nginx` configuration file located at `/etc/nginx/nginx.conf`.

To be more specific, let's take a look at the `nginx` configuration:
```bash
sudo cat /etc/nginx/nginx.conf
```
This will output the contents of the configuration file, which includes the server block for our web server:
```bash
http {
    ...
    server {
        listen 80;
        listen 443 ssl;
        ...
    }
}
```
This configuration tells `nginx` to listen on port 80 for HTTP requests and port 443 for HTTPS requests.

